research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.provenance.jsonl-12-{"id":"thm-cesaro-mean-theorem","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Summation_methods"],"rationale":"The source states Cauchy’s theorem that s_n tending to s implies its arithmetic averages tend to s, with the same quantifiers and conclusion. The numbered head-tail proof is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.provenance.jsonl-13-{"id":"thm-stolz-cesaro","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stolz%E2%80%93Ces%C3%A0ro_theorem"],"rationale":"The source gives the infinity-over-infinity Stolz-Cesaro theorem for a strictly increasing unbounded denominator and convergent difference quotient. The local version changes indexing and states the conclusion on a positive tail to admit b_0=0.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.provenance.jsonl-14-{"id":"cor-stolz-cesaro-zero-over-zero","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stolz%E2%80%93Ces%C3%A0ro_theorem"],"rationale":"The source gives the zero-over-zero Stolz-Cesaro form with a strictly decreasing positive denominator tending to zero. The local corollary uses zero-based indexing and expands the endpoint telescope.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.provenance.jsonl-15-{"id":"def-summability-matrix","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Toeplitz_matrix","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Summation_methods"],"rationale":"The sources define matrix summation transforms, regularity, and the row/column conditions. The local definition restricts to finite row support so every transform is a finite sum and makes admissible row bounds explicit.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.provenance.jsonl-16-{"id":"thm-silverman-toeplitz","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Toeplitz_matrix"],"rationale":"The Encyclopedia of Mathematics states the same three necessary and sufficient regularity conditions. The local theorem specializes to row-finite real matrices and numbers rows and columns from zero.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.provenance.jsonl-17-{"id":"cor-cesaro-matrix-is-regular","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Toeplitz_matrix","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Summation_methods"],"rationale":"The sources identify Cesaro averaging as a matrix summation method and give the three regularity conditions. The local corollary verifies them for the zero-based row-finite Cesaro matrix.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.provenance.jsonl-18-{"id":"rem-where-the-archimedean-hypothesis-is-needed","statement":"ai-generated","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"none","urls":[],"rationale":"Positive novelty determination: this is a repository-specific synthesis mapping the exact local five-property cycle, false statements, and Laurent dependencies to where ARCH is required. Each mathematical assertion is directly licensed by the named local items, but no external source can state this id-level scope map.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.provenance.jsonl:19:{"id":"fs-nested-intervals-implies-lub","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1101.5652"],"rationale":"Hall distinguishes Cantor completeness from Dedekind completeness outside the Archimedean setting. The local false universal deletes ARCH from the sourced implication and refutes that strengthening with the already-developed Laurent field.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.provenance.jsonl-20-{"id":"fs-cauchy-complete-implies-lub","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1101.5652"],"rationale":"Hall explicitly notes that sequential completeness is not sufficient for stronger completeness outside the Archimedean setting. The local false statement is that standard missing-ARCH strengthening with the Laurent field as witness.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.provenance.jsonl-21-{"id":"fs-cesaro-converse","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ces%C3%A0ro_summation","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grandi%27s_series"],"rationale":"The sources distinguish ordinary convergence from Cesaro summability and give the alternating classical pattern. The local false converse uses the zero-based alternating sequence and computes its means directly.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.provenance.jsonl-22-{"id":"ex-rational-function-field-order","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.hws.edu/eck/math331/guide2020/04-axioms-for-R.html"],"rationale":"The source defines positivity in the real rational-function field by the sign of the leading coefficient after normalizing the denominator, proves closure under addition and multiplication, and shows that t exceeds every canonical natural. This is exactly the local eventual-positive-infinity order; the local proof expands representative independence and the infinitesimal 1/t.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.provenance.jsonl-23-{"id":"ex-field-ordered-in-two-ways","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":[],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. The two orders on Q(sqrt(2)) obtained from the two real embeddings are a standard algebraic example, and the local proof also shows these are the only two. Searches surfaced informal exact discussions but no reputable source among the allowed classes for the full exact package; this is established mathematics, not an invented construction. ALPHA CONCURRENCE: The current construction is the canonical quadratic-field ordering argument: the two real embeddings send sqrt(2) to its two conjugates, and every field order is fixed by the sign of that generator. This is standard established ordered-field mathematics, merely packaged locally.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.provenance.jsonl-24-{"id":"ex-cauchy-complete-not-complete-field","statement":"ai-generated","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"none","urls":[],"rationale":"Positive novelty determination: the exact property inventory for this library’s K=R((t^{-1})) is a bespoke composition rather than an established named result. Its ordered-field, Cauchy-complete, non-Archimedean, non-LUB, shrinking-NIP, non-BW, and non-MCT assertions follow from the source-backed local Laurent and completeness results cited in the item.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.provenance.jsonl-25-{"id":"ex-cesaro-means-of-alternating","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ces%C3%A0ro_summation","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grandi%27s_series"],"rationale":"The sources give Cesaro averaging and the classical alternating pattern. The local item uses the term sequence 1,-1,... rather than Grandi partial sums, changes the Cesaro value accordingly to 0, and writes out the parity computation.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.provenance.jsonl-26-{"id":"ex-stolz-cesaro-applied","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Faulhaber%27s_formula","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stolz%E2%80%93Ces%C3%A0ro_theorem"],"rationale":"The local example composes two sourced results rather than reproducing one source statement: Faulhaber’s formula gives leading term n^(p+1)/(p+1), hence the displayed limit, and Stolz-Cesaro supplies the stated difference-quotient route. The local proof expands the power factorization.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.provenance.jsonl-27-{"id":"cex-q-not-dense-in-an-ordered-field","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archimedean_property","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ordered_field"],"rationale":"The sources state that a non-Archimedean ordered field has infinitesimal intervals containing no rational and give R(t) as the standard example. The local witness fixes the interval (0,1/t) and verifies it from the canonical embedding.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
